      "page_title": "JWT Anatomy & Signing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Three Dotted Parts",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "array",
          "spec": {
            "cells": [
              "header",
              ".",
              "payload",
              ".",
              "signature"
            ],
            "indices": true,
            "pointers": [],
            "highlight": [],
            "dim": [],
            "caption": "JWT"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "json",
          "lines": [
            "{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}",
            "{\"sub\":\"u_91\",\"exp\":1789000000}",
            "// signature covers header+payload"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "base64url parts joined by dots — self-contained, no lookup",
            "Header names the algorithm; payload holds the claims",
            "Standard claims: `iss` `sub` `aud` `exp` `nbf` `iat` `jti`",
            "`exp` and `aud` are the two people forget to verify"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Encoding, Not Encryption",
          "text": "Anyone can paste a JWT into a decoder and read the payload. Never put a password, PII, or any secret inside one."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Signing Choices",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Alg",
            "Key model",
            "Fits"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "HS256",
              "one shared secret",
              "single service"
            ],
            [
              "RS256/ES256",
              "private signs, public verifies",
              "multi-service"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "With HS256, every verifier can also mint tokens",
            "`alg: none` attack comes from trusting the header",
            "So does HS256/RS256 confusion",
            "Pin the expected algorithm server-side, always"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Core Trade-off"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Stateless verification is fast and scales flat",
            "You cannot revoke a token before `exp`",
            "Workarounds: 5–15 min access tokens",
            "Or a `jti` denylist, or a token-version column",
            "Each workaround reintroduces the state you removed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Bearer Headers Dodge CSRF",
          "text": "`Authorization: Bearer <jwt>` is immune by construction — the browser never attaches it automatically. Genuinely right for cross-service auth, mobile and native clients, third-party APIs, short-lived grants."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Axis",
            "Session",
            "Token"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "State",
              "server store",
              "nothing stored"
            ],
            [
              "Revocation",
              "instant delete",
              "needs a denylist"
            ],
            [
              "Scaling",
              "shared store",
              "stateless, flat"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Refresh Tokens & Token Storage",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Refresh Tokens",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Short access token in memory or a cookie",
            "Long-lived refresh token in an `HttpOnly` cookie",
            "Scope it narrow: `Path=/auth/refresh`",
            "Rotate: each refresh issues a new one, kills the old",
            "A reused old token means theft — kill the whole family"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where To Put It"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "localStorage",
                "lines": [
                  "popular bad advice",
                  "any XSS reads it",
                  "no `HttpOnly` available"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Secure cookie",
                "lines": [
                  "XSS protection back",
                  "takes on CSRF",
                  "CSRF is easier to defend"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Clock Skew",
          "items": [
            "Allow 30–60s leeway on `exp` and `nbf`",
            "Otherwise distributed servers reject valid tokens",
            "Symptom: intermittent 401s on one node only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Shape Of Each",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Session",
                "lines": [
                  "server-side store",
                  "opaque random ID",
                  "instant delete"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Token",
                "lines": [
                  "self-contained",
                  "signed claims",
                  "valid until `exp`"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Runtime Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Axis",
            "Session",
            "Token"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Per request",
              "~1ms Redis hop",
              "~0.1ms verify"
            ],
            [
              "Payload",
              "32–64 bytes",
              "200–800 bytes"
            ],
            [
              "Cross-domain",
              "cookie scoping",
              "any header"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Size Is Per Request",
          "text": "A 700-byte JWT rides on every single call, including static assets on the same host. The session cookie stays under 64 bytes forever."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Exposure",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Attack",
            "Session",
            "Token"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "XSS",
              "`HttpOnly` unreadable",
              "`localStorage` exposed"
            ],
            [
              "CSRF",
              "needs SameSite + token",
              "bearer is immune"
            ],
            [
              "Mobile",
              "awkward, no jar",
              "natural fit"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Verification, Rotation & Revocation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Sessions are simply easier to get right",
            "Tokens need refresh, rotation and key management",
            "Complexity is a real cost, not a style preference"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Picking One",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Server-rendered app or first-party SPA on one domain",
            "Then sessions in `__Host-` cookies, done",
            "Use tokens for multiple backend services",
            "Or a public API, native mobile apps, third-party integrations"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Verify Every Claim",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "jwt.verify(token, pubKey, {",
            "  algorithms: ['RS256'], // pinned",
            "  audience: 'api.example.com',",
            "  issuer: 'auth.example.com',",
            "  clockTolerance: 30 })  // skew"
          ],
          "note": "never trust the header's alg"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`jwt.decode()` in place of `verify()` — no signature check",
            "Letting the token's header choose the algorithm",
            "Skipping `aud` — a token for service A works on B",
            "Long `exp` \"to save refreshes\" — a stolen token lives on"
          ],
          "style": "cross"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Rotation In Practice",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              {
                "text": "POST /auth/refresh",
                "sub": "old RT cookie"
              },
              {
                "text": "look up family",
                "sub": "RT unused?"
              },
              {
                "text": "issue new pair",
                "sub": "AT + RT"
              },
              {
                "text": "mark old used",
                "sub": "reuse = theft",
                "ink": "red"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Event",
            "Server action"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Valid, unused RT",
              "rotate, new pair"
            ],
            [
              "Already-used RT",
              "revoke whole family"
            ],
            [
              "Expired RT",
              "force re-login"
            ],
            [
              "Password change",
              "bump token version"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Revocation Options"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Option",
            "Cost",
            "Kill delay"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "5–15 min `exp`",
              "more refreshes",
              "up to `exp`"
            ],
            [
              "`jti` denylist",
              "lookup per call",
              "instant"
            ],
            [
              "Version column",
              "DB read per call",
              "instant"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Stateless Has A Price",
          "text": "Every instant-revocation scheme adds a lookup back. If you need a lookup anyway, a plain session may be the simpler answer."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Hybrid & Session Mechanics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "The Common Hybrid",
          "text": "Session cookie for the browser UI, plus a short-lived JWT minted per request for service-to-service calls. Best of both, one auth surface."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const user = await store.get(req.cookies.sid)",
            "const svcJwt = sign({sub: user.id}, key,",
            "  { expiresIn: '60s' })  // internal hop only"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Session Mechanics",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Flow: verify credentials, create record, return the ID",
            "The cookie carries only an opaque random ID",
            "User, roles and expiry all live server-side",
            "Each request: look up the store, attach the user",
            "Logout deletes the record — the ID dies everywhere"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "const sid = crypto.randomBytes(32)",
            "  .toString('base64url')  // 256 bits",
            "req.session.regenerate()  // on login"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Timer",
            "Typical",
            "Guards"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Absolute",
              "12h",
              "stolen cookies"
            ],
            [
              "Idle",
              "30 min",
              "abandoned tabs"
            ],
            [
              "Rolling",
              "per request",
              "needs a cap"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Store Pitfalls",
          "items": [
            "In-memory stores break horizontal scaling",
            "Symptom: random logouts after every deploy",
            "Set TTL at the store level (Redis `EXPIRE`)",
            "Else dead sessions accumulate forever"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Signed Cookie Sessions",
          "text": "Rails and Flask style puts the data IN the cookie with an HMAC — no store to run, but no revocation either, and the 4KB cap bites fast."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Neither Fixes Bad Auth"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Rate-limit the login endpoint",
            "Hash with argon2 or bcrypt, never SHA-family",
            "Ship MFA",
            "These matter more than the transport choice"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Config Slips",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Missing `Secure`/`HttpOnly` because dev config got copied",
            "Assert flags in a startup check, not a code review",
            "Default `Path` swallowing the cookie set at `/api/login`",
            "It never reaches `/dashboard` — always `Path=/`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Dropped Without A Word",
          "text": "`SameSite=None` without `Secure` and the browser discards the cookie entirely — you get a console warning and nothing else."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Cookie Bugs That Fail Silently",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cookies That Refuse To Die"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Delete fails when `Domain`/`Path` differ from the original",
            "Cookie survives, user stays logged in",
            "Two same-name cookies: one host-only, one `Domain=`",
            "Server reads the wrong one — inspect the raw `Cookie` header",
            "Not the parsed map, which silently keeps one"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Flows That Break",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "OAuth callbacks and payment redirects die under `Strict`",
            "The return leg is a cross-site POST",
            "No regeneration on login = session fixation",
            "Attacker plants an ID, victim logs in, attacker owns it",
            "JWT in `localStorage` 'for convenience' — one XSS empties it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "fetch(url, { credentials: 'include' })",
            "// server must also send:",
            "// Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true",
            "// Access-Control-Allow-Origin: <exact>  # never *"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lax Is Not A GET Excuse",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Lax still rides on cross-site top-level GETs",
            "So `/delete?id=5` stays CSRF-able under Lax",
            "Never mutate state on a GET route",
            "Two-cookie pattern: Lax to read, Strict to write",
            "Chrome's 2-minute Lax+POST grace — never rely on it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Typos Fail Silently",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unknown attributes are ignored — `HttpOnley` sets nothing",
            "No error, no warning — the flag is just missing",
            "Cookie names are case-sensitive: `sid` ≠ `SID`",
            "Attribute names are not: `secure` = `Secure`"
          ],
          "style": "cross"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "curl -si https://site/login \\",
            "  | grep -i set-cookie",
            "# exactly what the browser stores"
          ],
          "note": "verify flags on the wire"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Debugging & Ship Checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Treat Values As Hostile",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cookies are fully attacker-controlled input",
            "Sign them, validate them, never SQL-interpolate them",
            "4KB overflow truncates or drops silently",
            "Happens when roles or flash messages bloat a signed session"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Symptom",
            "Likely cause"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Never stored",
              "`None` without `Secure`"
            ],
            [
              "Not sent on fetch",
              "no `credentials`"
            ],
            [
              "Wrong user",
              "duplicate name"
            ],
            [
              "Gone after login",
              "default `Path`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Delete It Properly"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "js",
          "lines": [
            "res.clearCookie('__Host-sid',",
            "  { path: '/', secure: true })",
            "// name + Domain + Path must all match",
            "await store.destroy(sid)  // server side too"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Debug Toolkit",
          "items": [
            "DevTools, Application, Cookies — shows every flag",
            "`curl -v` for what the wire really carries",
            "`document.cookie` hides `HttpOnly` — that is the point",
            "Network tab's Cookies pane logs rejections with a reason"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Check Chrome First",
          "text": "Chrome states exactly why it dropped a cookie in the Network tab. Read that before blaming your server code."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Ship Checklist"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`__Host-` prefix, `Secure`, `HttpOnly`",
            "`SameSite=Lax` or `Strict`, `Path=/`, no `Domain`",
            "Absolute plus idle timeout, both",
            "Regenerate on login, delete server-side on logout"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "One Line",
          "text": "Cookies are automatic, scoped by site not origin, and attacker-visible — so sign them, flag them, scope them tight, and keep the real state on the server."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Leading-Dot Domain Myth",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Legacy Flag",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The `Domain` attribute is a legacy mechanism. Modern browsers normalize it to match host-only behavior.",
          "frame": "dashed",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`Domain=.example.com` is the old syntax",
            "Browsers strip the leading dot automatically",
            "Result is identical to `Domain=example.com`",
            "No functional difference in modern engines"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Normalization Process",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "http",
          "lines": [
            "Set-Cookie: sid=abc; Domain=.ex.com",
            "// Browser normalizes to:",
            "Set-Cookie: sid=abc; Domain=ex.com",
            "// Behavior is identical today"
          ],
          "note": "Leading dot is stripped"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Input",
            "Normalized",
            "Status"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              ".example.com",
              "example.com",
              "Identical"
            ],
            [
              "example.com",
              "example.com",
              "Identical"
            ],
            [
              ".sub.example.com",
              "sub.example.com",
              "Identical"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Why It Matters",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Old tutorials still show the dot syntax",
            "Confuses developers about scope differences",
            "Actually, there is no difference",
            "Treat both forms as the same cookie"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "Normalization rules"
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Domain Scope & Subdomain Traps",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Legacy vs Modern",
          "text": "The leading dot was a historical artifact. Today, `Domain=.example.com` and `Domain=example.com` produce the exact same cookie scope and behavior."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Best Practice",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Prefer omitting `Domain` for host-only",
            "Use `Domain` only for subdomain sharing",
            "Never rely on the dot for security",
            "Scope tight: use the smallest domain needed"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Common Mistake",
          "items": [
            "Thinking `.example.com` is more secure",
            "It is not — same scope as `example.com`",
            "Security comes from `Secure` and `HttpOnly`",
            "Not from the presence of a dot"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Use __Host- For Auth",
          "text": "`__Host-` forbids `Domain` and forces `Secure` + `Path=/`, so no subdomain can ever overwrite your session cookie."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. What A Host May Set",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Set on",
            "Domain=",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "app.ex.com",
              "(none)",
              "host-only"
            ],
            [
              "app.ex.com",
              "ex.com",
              "all subdomains"
            ],
            [
              "a.ex.com",
              "b.ex.com",
              "**rejected**"
            ],
            [
              "app.ex.com",
              "com",
              "rejected (PSL)"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. The Subdomain Trap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "XSS on `blog.` reads a `Domain=example.com` session",
            "Cookie tossing: a subdomain shadows the parent's cookie",
            "Port is not scope: `:3000` and `:8080` share a jar"
          ],
          "style": "cross"
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "Normalization rules"
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of HTTP COOKIES & WEB AUTH. Your session cookie is one missing attribute away from being someone else's. This field manual walks every `Set-Cookie` attribute, why `Path` silently eats your login, how a compromised subdomain can overwrite a cookie it should never touch, and where SameSite actually stops CSRF versus where it just looks like it does. Ends with the session-vs-JWT table and a ship checklist you can paste into your auth PR.",
